// SHA-256's compression as functions of its context (FIPS 180-4 §6.2.2,
// issues/sha256-context-type.md, docs/layout-design.md §9.5 item 4): the
// schedule, the working variables, the sixty-four rounds and the new hash
// value, each a `mut self` method of `Sha256` (`sha256_context.hthr`)
// that reads and stores the context's words by name and index. Nothing
// here writes an offset or a width: `s.schedule` at `t.toNat()` is the
// accessor's checked element where the declaration lays it.
//
// These are the linked ones: `sha256_compress` copies the block's bytes
// into the schedule (`List::sha256_take_block`, a function over the span)
// and runs `Sha256::run`'s code on the span, and the stream's functions
// call the same code through `List::sha256_run`.
//
// What each publishes is the generator's: `Sha256::run::bytes`, the code's
// function over the span, and `Sha256::run::runs_held`, that where the
// span holds a context the code computes `Sha256::run` of it and leaves
// the span holding what `Sha256::run` leaves. Nothing is proved by hand.
//
// A word is 32 bits in the context and 64 in the code, so the word
// functions (`sha256_round.hthr`) take each `zero_extend`ed and their
// results are `truncate`d where stored. An element at a word index is
// read with a value for past the end (`vector_get(…, 0)`): the code tests
// the index, so no method here owes a bound.
//
// The schedule and the working variables' start are `sha256_schedule.hthr`
// and a round is `sha256_working.hthr`: three modules, so that no one
// module's elaboration is all of them
// (issues/runtime-tests-cold-build-past-rail.md).
//
// What the `runtime` generator reads (issues/runtime-generator-imports.md).
import function_ir_lower;
